   "tags": [
    "Episode 83"
   ],
   "image": "eniac.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A history special. Six women programmed the first general-purpose electronic computer in 1945 and were left out of the photos for decades.",
   "date": "2026-08-20",
   "content": "<!-- wp:audio -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-audio\"><audio controls src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg\"></audio><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Stand-in audio: “The ENIAC Programmers”, told by Megan Smith for the White House (public domain). Replace with your episode file.</figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:audio -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Chapters</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"is-style-chapters\"} -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list is-style-chapters\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg#t=0\">0:00</a> Intro</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg#t=200\">3:20</a> Philadelphia, 1945</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg#t=1200\">20:00</a> Programming by plugging cables</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg#t=2100\">35:00</a> The 1946 demonstration</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/The_ENIAC_Programmers_%28As_Told_By_U.S._Chief_Technology_Officer_Megan_Smith%29.ogg#t=2800\">46:40</a> What we got wrong in our first draft</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Show notes</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} -->\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Added</h4>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"is-style-diff-added\"} -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list is-style-diff-added\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ENIAC\">ENIAC on Wikipedia</a>, with the names: Kay McNulty, Betty Jennings, Betty Snyder, Marlyn Wescoff, Fran Bilas and Ruth Lichterman.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} -->\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fixed</h4>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"is-style-diff-fixed\"} -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list is-style-diff-fixed\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The first cut said ENIAC was the first computer. It was the first general-purpose electronic one. Kwame caught it.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
